    "title": "Denver police begin month-long vehicle registration crackdown - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Shaul Turner No media credit provided. ,\n2 hours ago\nDENVER (KDVR) — Denver police are cracking down on drivers on the road with expired plates in a campaign that begins on Saturday and continues through Dec. 15.\nIn 2024, Denver’s police department announced a policy change limiting low-level traffic stops, but after receiving emails and hearing an outcry at community events, the department is heating up its effort to crack down on drivers with expired registrations.\nNew court filings reveal extensive case against suspect in Cherry Creek car break-ins “I feel like people would be more accountable if they were registered,” one driver told FOX31.\nDenver Police Chief Ron Thomas tells FOX31 the focus should always be on safety for every driver on the road.\n“We want to see people driving safely on the roads, and so that’s why I want their focus to be mostly on those safety violations,” he said.\nDuring a similar enforcement period from mid-April to mid-May this year, DPD issued citations for 656 vehicle registration violations.\n“What’s more important than the number of citations that we write are the number of people that go and actually register their vehicles,” said Thomas.\nThe Department of Motor Vehicles states that newly purchased or acquired vehicles must be registered in person at DMV offices , while registration renewals must be done online or at a kiosk. While vehicle registrations have a 30-day grace period after expiration, temporary plates do not.\nCopyright 2025 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to FOX31 Denver.\n2",
